Legacy Problem: too many devices use the existing standard. Changing one aspect of a widely sold product would require a major change in the number of products . New parts wouldn't work with old equipment. Basically what Apple lovves to profit from haha

This chapter delves into how we interact with something that we have never seen before, which should hopefully involve perciving the affordances and signifiers, as well as using background knowledge to bridge the gaps between the preset situation and past experiences. That sounds like assuming the power button on a tv or monitor could either be on the bottom right side, especially if the features are clearly labelled in that area facing the user, but that's not the case for a Mac so you search the whole monitor because it just has to turn on somehow. Their power button is on the center bottom for some strange reason, with no label. Very unique, to me at least. 
Maybe it's like when the two retangular buttons side-by-side on a phone or Ipad are widely known to raise or lower the volume. To keep things simple, it would make sense for future models to continue with that feature. Alternatively, there are little learned features to navigate through Iphones more efficiently, but as far I know they don't work on my mom's Samsung, which was embarrassing when I wanted to help her with some phone and wifi stuff recently.

