Roger Schank and Bob Abelson proposed that we follow "scripts" to guide the sequence of our behavior

Semantic Constraints: semantics is the study of meaning. these rely on the meaning of the situation to control possible actions. Basically, it's why the implementations we come up with actually do make sense, like needing a windsheild to protect driver's faces or where a driver on a motorcyle would sit. Meaningful and sensible connections.

Logical Constraints: the logical relationship between the spacial and functional layout of components and thinngs they affect or are affected by. Left switch controls left light, right switch controls right light type of logic. "natural mapping"

