Task analysis: method to describe how useful designs begin with careful observations of how the tasks being supported are actually performed, followed by a good-fitting design process. Sounds like intelligent, streamlined design. Also part of human centered design

Natural mapping is pretty self explanatory

Norman mentions activity-centered controls and spacial mapping, specifically of an auditorium on page 140. This auditorium has mapping for activitis like "video", "full light", "lecture". This reminded me of how my headphones has a couple different modes, like gaming or music mode, and as far as I can tell they all sound the same and do the same thing haha

We lock in on page 143. Lock-Ins keep an operation active, preventing someone from prematurely stopping it. This is present on many standard computeres, where if something needs to be saved it will prompt the user to save before shutting off. 

Lock outs: for safety, inhibit interaction. like the tops on pill bottles.

Standardization: the fundamental principle of desperation, just design everything same way so people only have to learn once. 

Norman talks about common sound signifiers starting at page 155: click when the bolt on a door slides home, roaring sound when there's a hole in car muffler, tea kettle whistle

The last bit of standardization versus indiividualization seems important to consider. If you make to many changes, the viewers, users, and listeners will get confused and not know what they're interacting with. So do what works, and add enough individualization to stand apart from the crowd (from a marketing standpoint) without breaking too many conventions and becoming un-interactable.

userful chapter, good info. 

